## Uniqueness of extension IDs ## {#uniqueness-of-extension-ids}

An <dfn>extension ID</dfn> is an opaque string that identifies a WebExtension. Every loaded WebExtension has an extension ID.

A [=user agent=] must not have two different currently-loaded WebExtensions with the same extension ID in the same profile. Loading a WebExtension whose extension ID matches an already-loaded WebExtension is an update of that WebExtension, not the addition of a second, distinct one.

<div class=issue>
Should this specification define a single extension-ID derivation algorithm every browser can
adopt, or a deliberately loose, implementation-defined definition?

Chrome, Firefox, and Safari produce the extension ID in three fundamentally different ways:

* Chrome derives the ID deterministically by hashing the extension's
    packaging public key (the first 16 bytes of the SHA-256 hash of the
    DER-encoded public key, re-encoded using the 16-letter alphabet
    `a`-`p` instead of hexadecimal, giving a fixed 32-character lowercase
    string). An extension loaded unpacked, with no key yet assigned, gets
    an ID hashed from its filesystem path instead, which is stable across
    reloads of that same path but changes if the extension is moved or
    later packed with a key.
* Firefox takes the ID directly from the `id` property of the manifest's
    `browser_specific_settings.gecko` key (or the deprecated
    `applications.gecko.id`), authored by the developer. The accepted
    forms are a GUID wrapped in braces (`{8-4-4-4-12 hex digits}`) or an
    email-like string (`[a-z0-9-._]*@[a-z0-9-._]+`), matched
    case-insensitively. If the key is omitted and the package is signed,
    the ID is taken from the signing certificate instead; if the key is
    omitted, the package is unsigned, and the load is a temporary
    development load, an ID is generated that is explicitly not stable
    across restarts.
* Safari does not read any ID from the manifest at all. The identifier is
    a property the embedding application assigns to the extension out of
    band, defaulting to a freshly generated random value if the
    application does not supply one. Nothing about that default ties it
    to the extension's manifest, package, or signing key, and unlike the
    other two browsers it need not fit any particular character
    set.

The loose option is an opaque, [=user agent=]-assigned string meeting only a minimum
uniqueness and stability bar, leaving derivation itself as an implementation detail.
</div>

The accepted character set, length, and case-sensitivity of an extension ID are implementation-defined.

<div class="example">
Depending on the [=user agent=], an extension ID may look like `bmnlcjabdbnaibedpokdopcffdilbkco` (Chrome's derived form), `{daf44bf7-a45e-4450-979c-91cf07434c3a}` or `my-extension@example.org` (Firefox's authored forms), or any other unique string an embedding application assigns.
</div>

An extension ID must be stable for the lifetime of a given installation of a WebExtension: reloading, restarting the [=user agent=], or restarting the device must not change it. Whether the same extension ID is produced again for a *separate* installation of the same WebExtension source, on the same or a different machine, or when loading the same source both unpacked and packed, depends on how each [=user agent=] derives its IDs, and is not required by this specification.

<div class=issue>
Must an extension ID survive more than the installation that created it?

* Chrome: derived from the packaging key, so it is the same for every
    install of the same signed package, on any machine.
* Firefox: authored in the manifest, so it is the same for every install
    while the manifest is unchanged.
* Safari: a freshly generated identifier is not guaranteed to survive
    even a relaunch, unless the embedding application persists it and
    reassigns it before each load.

</div>

An [=extension origin=]'s extension-specific [=host=] is not necessarily the extension ID.

<div class=issue>
Must the [=extension origin=] [=host=] be the [=extension ID=]?

* Chrome's [=extension origin=] [=host=] is the extension ID directly.
* Firefox's [=extension origin=] [=host=] is a separate, randomly generated
    identifier, unrelated to the extension ID, generated once per
    (profile, extension) pair and persisted for the life of that
    profile. This is deliberate, to avoid exactly the observability the
    general rule below describes.
* Safari's [=extension origin=] [=host=] defaults to the extension ID, but
    the embedding application may set the two independently, so they are
    not guaranteed to match.

Exposing the extension ID as the [=extension origin=] [=host=] is not
necessary, and doing so forecloses Firefox's anti-fingerprinting design.
This bears directly on how observable the
extension ID is from a web page, including through a
[=web accessible resource=]; see
[w3c/webextensions#868](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/868)
for a concrete leak of this kind via declarativeNetRequest redirects.
Tracked at
[w3c/webextensions#238](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/238) and
[#896](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/896), and discussed at the
2025-11-10 TPAC and 2026-04-09 London face-to-face sessions; no resolution
reached as of the newest minutes checked.
</div>

The extension ID is available to a [=privileged extension context=] and [=content script context=] of the WebExtension it identifies. An extension may also learn the extension ID of another, cooperating extension through APIs that name it explicitly, such as [`externally_connectable`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-externally_connectable).

Where a [=user agent=]'s [=extension origin=] [=host=] is the [=extension
ID=], a web page that can load a resource from that origin, such as a
[=web accessible resource=], observes the extension ID as that
resource's [=host=].
